{"name":"Magnolia Blossom","hex":"#f4e7ce","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"magnolia","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A tree or shrub in any species of the genus Magnolia, many with large flowers and simple leaves.","The flower of a magnolia tree."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a creamy white colour, like that of some magnolia flowers."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/magnolia"},{"term":"blossom","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A flower, especially one indicating that a fruit tree is fruiting;  a mass of such flowers.","The state or season of producing such flowers."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To have, or open into, blossoms; to bloom.","To begin to thrive or flourish."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blossom"}],"entries":[{"title":"Magnolia","blocks":[{"text":"Magnolia is a large genus of about 210 to 340 flowering plant species in the subfamily Magnolioideae of the family Magnoliaceae. The natural range of Magnolia species is disjunct, with a main center in east, south and southeast Asia and a secondary center in South America, Central America, the West Indies, and some species in eastern North America."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Magnolia_sieboldii_flower_1.jpg/330px-Magnolia_sieboldii_flower_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnolia","wikidata":"Q157017"},{"title":"Blossom","blocks":[{"text":"In botany, blossoms are the flowers of stone fruit trees and of some other plants with a similar appearance that flower profusely for a period of time in spring."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Badamwari_Flower_Series_2.png/330px-Badamwari_Flower_Series_2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blossom","wikidata":"Q2047589"},{"title":"Magnolia (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Magnolia is a color named after the flowering plant of the genus Magnolia. As magnolias have flowers of more than one color, mainly cream or pale purple, magnolia may refer to different colors in different countries."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Magnolia_wieseneri.jpg/330px-Magnolia_wieseneri.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnolia_(color)","wikidata":"Q6731851"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Magnoliafruitopen.JPG/500px-Magnoliafruitopen.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Magnoliafruitopen.JPG/1280px-Magnoliafruitopen.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Magnoliafruitopen.JPG/500px-Magnoliafruitopen.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMagnoliafruitopen.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Magnolia_biondii.jpg/500px-Magnolia_biondii.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Magnolia_biondii.jpg/1280px-Magnolia_biondii.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Magnolia_biondii.jpg/500px-Magnolia_biondii.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An anatomical diagram of the flower of Magnolia biondii","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMagnolia_biondii.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Magnolia_Fruit_%28South_America%29.JPG/500px-Magnolia_Fruit_%28South_America%29.JPG?utm_s